course: arts
lesson: 20
title: Photography and phone cameras
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A camera is a box that catches light: the lens gathers it and the sensor (or, in old cameras, the film) records it, so a photo is really a picture of light.
explain: Our eyes adjust to bright and dark at the same time, but cameras struggle, which is why a glowing sunset can come out dark and dull, and why people with the sun behind them come out as black shapes called silhouettes.
explain: Good photos come more from knowing light and angles than from an expensive phone: move closer instead of zooming, put the light on people's faces, crouch to a child's or a pet's eye level, and try the rule of thirds instead of always aiming at the middle.
explain: Photos have a history and a kindness side: the first photographs needed hours to take, old film cameras held only 24 or 36 shots, and today you should ask before posting a picture of someone.
explain: People say it their way: "bruh" is a groan, "mad blurry" means very blurry, and in Britain "I look a state" means I look a mess.
fact: Exposure is how much light a photo lets in, and focus is which part looks sharp; tapping the screen to choose them helps.
fact: The rule of thirds splits a picture into a 3 by 3 grid and places important things on the lines or where they cross; many phones can show this grid.
fact: Backlighting means bright light is behind the subject, so the camera makes the people dark; turning so the light is on their faces fixes it.
fact: Before digital cameras, photos were taken on film that had to be developed with chemicals in a dark room, and a roll often had only 24 or 36 pictures, so people took fewer, more careful shots, which many photographers say teaches you a lot.
fact: Taking and sharing photos of people without asking can upset them and sometimes break rules; consent means agreeing, and you should ask before you post.
fact: The "Tank Man" photo from Beijing in 1989 shows an unknown man standing in front of a line of tanks; it captured a real moment, and photographers risked a lot to take and save it.
fact: Digital zoom just enlarges pixels and loses detail; getting closer or using a real zoom lens gives sharper pictures.
fact: A pixel is one tiny dot of a digital picture.
fact: Photographing children and animals from their eye level makes the picture feel closer and more natural.
fact: The oldest surviving camera photograph, by Nicéphore Niépce in France around 1826 or 1827, needed hours or even days of exposure.
fact: Early portraits needed people to keep still for a while, so smiles were hard to hold, and serious faces were also the fashion.
fact: Photos take up storage; photographers pick their best few and delete the rest.
exercise: Max's sunset photo looks dark and dull. Why? | Cameras struggle with bright and dark at once, unlike eyes | His phone is broken and must be replaced with a brand new one today | Sunsets cannot be photographed at all | The sunset was much darker than he remembers it being
exercise: Amara always puts her friend in the middle. What does the rule of thirds suggest? | Placing things on the lines of a 3 by 3 grid | Always placing things in the exact centre of the picture | Cutting the photo into three pieces | Taking three photos of everything, just to be safe
exercise: Tom's family come out as black shapes. What happened? | They were backlit, with the sun behind them | The phone added a ninja filter all by itself | The camera ran out of colour | They were standing in the rain, which made them dark
exercise: What fixes backlit faces? | Turning so the light falls on their faces | Moving much further away from them | Zooming in as far as possible | Taking the photo at night with no light at all
exercise: Grandma Liu waited a week for her photos. Why? | Film had to be developed with chemicals first | Photos were sent by slow post straight from the camera itself | Cameras took a week to click | People only looked at photos on Sundays
exercise: How many pictures did a film roll often hold? | 24 or 36 | Thousands | Just one shot | Exactly 100
exercise: Ayesha posts a photo of her friend without asking. What should she have done? | Asked before posting | Posted it much faster | Added a nicer filter first | Posted it on a different app
exercise: Oliver asks if the Tank Man photo was staged. What is true? | It captured a real moment in Beijing in 1989 | It was set up by actors for a film about China | It was drawn by a cartoonist | It was made by AI last year and shared online
exercise: Lucy zooms all the way and the bird is fuzzy. Why? | Digital zoom enlarges pixels and loses detail | Birds always look blurry in photos, whatever you do | Her finger was on the lens | The bird was moving too slowly for the camera to see it
exercise: What is a pixel? | One tiny dot of a digital picture | A very small camera used by spies | A type of film roll | A little light on the back of the phone
exercise: Kyle's aunt crouches to his sister's eye level. Why is it better? | Shooting at eye level feels closer and more natural | Crouching makes the phone take sharper photos than standing | Children only smile at low cameras | Standing up is not allowed at the beach
exercise: Dasha hears the first photo took hours. Who made the oldest surviving camera photo? | Nicéphore Niépce in France, around 1826 or 1827 | Leonardo da Vinci in Italy, around the year 1500 | Andy Warhol in the 1960s | A phone company in 2007, with the first smartphone
exercise: Why did people in very old photos rarely smile? | Long exposures made smiles hard to hold, and serious faces were the fashion | They were all sad all the time in the old days, because life was so hard | Smiling was against the law | Cameras could not show teeth
exercise: Jacob's phone says "storage full" after 200 cat photos. What do photographers do? | Pick the best few and delete the rest | Keep every single photo for ever, however many | Buy a new phone every week | Stop taking photos for good and draw the cat
exercise: What does exposure mean? | How much light a photo lets in | Which part of the photo is sharp | How many photos fit on the phone | Who is allowed to see the photo
test: Your mate goes, "my phone's trash, a new one would fix all my photos." What matters more? | Knowing light and angles | Buying the newest, priciest phone | Using more and more filters | Taking lots of photos much faster
test: What would happen if you photographed your friend at noon with the bright sun right behind her? | She would probably come out as a dark silhouette | She would look brighter and clearer than ever | The photo would be all white | Nothing at all would change in the photo
test: Ayesha's friend shouts, "Delete it, I look a state!" ("a state" means a mess). What is the right thing? | Delete it, because the person in a photo has a say | Keep it, because Ayesha took it, so it belongs to her | Post it again with a filter | Send it to more people so they can decide
test: Leo says "Bruh, I'd die" about waiting a week for film. What lesson can film teach? | Fewer, careful photos can teach you more | Waiting always makes the photos worse | Film photos were always blurry | Film cameras took a thousand shots on every roll
test: A cartoon shows a phone zooming into a bird a mile away perfectly sharp. What is wrong? | Digital zoom loses detail and goes blurry | Nothing, digital zoom is always perfect | Birds cannot be photographed | Phones cannot zoom at all, not even a little
test: Lucy says it's "mad blurry" (very blurry). What should she try next time? | Move closer instead of zooming | Zoom in even more next time | Shake the phone | Shout at the bird to keep still
test: Dasha asks, "Hours? People had to sit still that long?" Which is true? | The very first photograph needed hours or even days of light | Photos have always been instant, from the very first one | Old cameras took a picture in one second | The first photo was taken in 2007
test: Your cousin says the Tank Man photo "must be fake because it's so dramatic". What is true? | Many news photos capture real moments in a split second | All famous photos are set up | Dramatic photos are always made by AI or by actors | Newspapers never print real photos of real events
test: What would most likely happen if Amara turned on the grid and put her friend on a side line? | The photo might look more interesting | The photo would be cut in half by the line | Her friend would vanish | The phone would refuse to take it at all
test: Jacob shouts "Oh come on, mate!" at "storage full". What is a good habit? | Choose the best shots and delete the rest | Keep all 200 for ever | Never photograph the cat again | Buy a bigger cat that fits in fewer photos
test: Kyle takes photos looking down at his little sister. What would make them better? | Getting down to her eye level | Standing on a chair to go higher | Zooming in from far away across the room | Turning off the camera to watch her
exam: Identify the principal cause of the appearance of persons as dark silhouettes in a photograph taken outdoors. | The placing of a bright source of light behind the subjects | A defect in the manufacture of the camera | The insufficiency of colour within the sensor of the camera employed | The presence of rain at the time of exposure
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning digital zoom. | It enlarges the pixels of the image and thereby diminishes detail | It increases the detail of the image without limit of any kind | It employs an additional lens in every instance | It has no effect whatsoever upon the image
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the obligations of a person intending to publish a photograph of another? | The consent of the person depicted ought first to be obtained | The photographer alone is entitled to decide without enquiry | Publication is permitted provided a filter is applied | No consideration of any kind is required
exam: Ascertain the approximate date and author of the oldest surviving photograph taken with a camera. | Nicéphore Niépce, in France, around 1826 or 1827 | Leonardo da Vinci, in Florence, Italy, around the year 1500 | Andy Warhol, in New York, in the United States, in the 1960s | An unknown maker of mobile telephones, in the year 2007
